\noindent The latter vanishes due to the relation in Lemma \ref{lem:rel11}.


    Next, we assume $\{x,y\} \not\subseteq A$, say $x \in [n] \setminus A$. Then $\theta_{xy}$ can be viewed as the $y$-th section of $\pi_{x}: \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n} \rightarrow \overline{\mathcal M}_{2, [n] \setminus x}$, up to replacing $y$ by $p$ in the source. Let $\sigma_{p,y} : \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,([n]\setminus xy) \cup p} \rightarrow \overline{\mathcal M}_{2, [n] \setminus x}$ be the isomorphism that maps $p$ to $y$ and all other markings to themselves. It follows that, in $H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2, ([n] \setminus xy) \cup p})$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \theta_{xy}^\ast(V_A) = \theta_{xy}^\ast \pi_{x}^\ast \pi_{[n] \setminus (A \cup x)}^\ast(V) = \sigma_{p,y}^\ast \pi_{[n] \setminus (A \cup x)}^\ast(V) = \begin{cases}
            \pi_{[n] \setminus (A \cup x)}^\ast(V) & \text{if } y \in A\\
            \pi_{([n] \cup p) \setminus (A \cup xy)}^\ast(V) & \text{if } y \in [n] \setminus A.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    The cardinality of $([n] \setminus xy) \cup p$ is $n-1$, so by induction on $n$ we find that, for any fixed $\{x,y\} \subseteq [n]$, the sum
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{A \subseteq [n] \setminus \{x,y\}} \pi_{([n] \cup p) \setminus (A \cup xy)}^\ast(V) \subseteq H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2, ([n] \setminus xy) \cup p})
    \end{equation*}
    is direct and intersects $\sum_{|A \cap \{x,y\}| = 1} \pi_{[n] \setminus (A \cup xy)}^\ast(V) \subseteq H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2, ([n] \setminus xy) \cup p})$ trivially. Hence, the sum
    \begin{equation*}
        \sum_{A \subseteq [n] \setminus \{x,y\}} V_A \subseteq H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n})
    \end{equation*}
    is also direct and intersects $\sum_{|A \cap \{x,y\}| \geq 1} V_A \subseteq H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n})$ trivially. Running over all $2$-element subsets $\{x,y\} \subseteq [n]$ then yields the desired result.

\label{theorem:Genus2AnyMarkings}
   (a) We have
    \begin{equation*}
        H^{13,2}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n}) = S_\omega^{13,2}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n})
    \end{equation*}
    for all $n$.

    (b) Moreover, the only relations among the natural generators are those pulled back from $\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,12}$, i.e., the only relations are the pullbacks under forgetful maps of the known relations among the $M_{ij}$.

The strategy to prove part (a) has already been outlined in Section~\ref{section:ReductionsTo14Markings}. Ignoring restriction (R8) in Figure~\ref{fig:gentable}, a simple count of the number of the natural generators in genus $g = 2$ for $n = 14$ markings gives $239239$ (see Section \ref{sec:dimcounts} for general formulas counting generators).
Meanwhile, the number of relations among the $\mathcal D'_{B,ij}$ is $55 \cdot \binom{14}{12} = 5005$ by Lemma~\ref{lemma:pullbacksMij}. The difference is precisely the number $234234 = \dim H^{13,2}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,14})$ discussed in Section~\ref{subsection:StrategyGenus2Markings14}. 
For part (a), 
it is therefore enough to show that, modulo the $\mathcal D'$ classes, the remaining natural generators are linearly independent when $n = 14$.

Meanwhile, for part (b), we must show that, for any number of markings, the only relations are those pulled back from $\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,12}$. By Lemma \ref{lemma:pullbacksMij}, the only relations in the subspace spanned by pullbacks of $M_{ij}$ are the pullbacks of relations from $\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,12}$. To show there are no more relations, it suffices to show that, modulo the $\mathcal D'$ classes, the remaining natural generators are linearly independent for all $n$.

To do so, suppose we are given a linear relation among the natural generators of the form
\begin{align} 
&\sum s^a_{B,X,Y} \mathcal S^a_{B,X,Y} + \sum n_{B,X,Y} \mathcal N'_{B,X,Y} + \sum_{|X| \in \{1,2\}} \ell_{B,X} \mathcal L_{B,X} \notag \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad  + \sum_{|X| \geq 3} \ell'_{B,X} \mathcal L'_{B,X} + \sum_{|X| \geq 3} d_{B,X} \mathcal D_{B,X}= \sum_{|X| = 2} d'_{B,X} \mathcal D'_{B,X}. \label{eq:rel}
\end{align}
Our goal is to show that $s^a_{B,X,Y} = n_{B,X,Y} = \ell_{B,X} = \ell'_{B,X} = d_{B,X} = 0$.

The basic strategy is to pull back this linear relation along different boundary divisors, obtaining relations in various K\"unneth components of the boundary divisor. We indicate the K\"unneth components under consideration by writing them above the stable graph corresponding to the boundary divisor under consideration.
Ranging over different pullback maps and K\"unneth components allows us to show that different coefficients in the linear relation \eqref{eq:rel} must vanish. When we have shown that the coefficient of a certain generator is zero, we will say that this generator has been eliminated.

We proceed by computing pullbacks in a
carefully chosen order. The first five steps show the vanishing of the coefficients $\ell_{B,X}$, and vanishing of the coefficients for the generators with a rational tail with exactly $2$ markings on the rational component. Step (6) eliminates all remaining (unmodified) natural generators that have a rational tail. The final Steps (8)--(9) inductively eliminate the remaining side generators, all unmodified double edge generators and all modified loop generators.

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item We pull back to  

