By \cite[Theorem~8]{CanningRarelyGorenstein} the even degree cohomology of $\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}$ is tautological so it remains to show that its odd degree cohomology vanishes. Since $\dim \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}=19$, Poincaré duality reduces this to proving $H^k(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})=0$ for $k=13,15,17,19$. By \cite[Theorem~1.7]{CLPW24polynomial}, we have $H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})=0$. We treat the remaining degrees case by case.

    Let $k=15$. By Lemma \ref{lem:h=s}, $H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})=S_\omega^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})$. When $(g,n)=(5,7)$ there are not enough marked points to obtain a nonzero generator in Figure \ref{fig:gentable}.

    Let $k=17$. Equation \eqref{eq:oddsurj} gives the surjection
    \[
    H^{15}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal M_{5,7}}) \to H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}).
    \]
Each separating boundary divisor is the image of a gluing map $\overline{\mathcal M}_{a,A \cup s} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{5-a,A^c \cup t} \to \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}$ where $|A \cup s|, |A^c \cup t| \leq 8$.
For each separating divisor, the Künneth decomposition, together with the restrictions on the number of marked points, gives
\[
H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{a,A \cup s} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{5-a,A^c \cup t})=0.
\]
Thus the only possible nonzero contribution comes from the image of $H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,[7] \cup \{s,t\}})$ under the self-gluing map. By Lemma \ref{lem:h=s}, we refer to Figure \ref{fig:gentable} which gives the single generator
