where we identify $s$ and $t$ with the last two markings of $[9]$.
Consider its image under the self-gluing map
\begin{equation}\label{eq:eta gluing M_4,9 to M_5,7}
\eta_2: \overline{\mathcal M}_{4,[7] \cup \{s,t\} } \to \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7},
\end{equation}
which glues the marked points $s,t$. Applying Corollary \ref{cor:graph-averaging-vanishing} shows the pushforward vanishes in $H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})$.

Let $k=19$. We have the surjection
\[
H^{17}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal M_{5,7}}) \to H^{19}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}).
\]
We begin by analyzing the separating boundary divisors. 
First consider $\overline{\mathcal M}_{a,A \cup s} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{5-a,A^c \cup t} \to \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}$ with $a = 5$. In this case, $|A^c| \geq 2$, so $|A \cup s| \leq 6$. By the smaller cases of Theorem \ref{thm:rh} already proved, the cohomology of the $\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,m}$ with $m \leq 6$ is tautological (see proof of 
Lemma \ref{lem: implies taut for many g,n}). Hence, these separating boundary divisors make no contribution.

Next consider $\overline{\mathcal M}_{a,A \cup s} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{5-a,A^c \cup t} \to \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}$ with $a = 4$. In this case, $|A \cup s| \leq 8$. We already showed that the $\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,m}$ with $m \leq 8$ have tautological cohomology, so these separating boundary divisors likewise make no contribution. 

Finally when $a = 3$, \cite[Table~3]{CanningRarelyGorenstein} shows that all odd degree cohomology of $\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,n}$ and $\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,n}$ vanishes for $n\leq 8$. Consequently, none of the separating boundary divisors contributes to $H^{19}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})$.

It remains to analyze the image of $H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,[7]\cup\{s,t\}}) \to H^{19}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})$. Using \eqref{eq:oddsurj}, the map
\[
H^{15}(\widetilde{\partial\mathcal M_{4,9}}) \to H^{17}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,9})
\]
is surjective. The normalization of the boundary is
\begin{align*}
\widetilde{\partial\mathcal M_{4,9}}=\bigcup \overline{\mathcal M}_{4,Y_1\cup \{r_1\}} \times\overline{\mathcal M}_{0,Y_1^c\cup \{r_2\}} \sqcup \bigcup \overline{\mathcal M}_{3,Y_2\cup \{v_1\}} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{1,Y_2^c\cup \{v_2\}} \\
\sqcup \bigcup \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3\cup \{w_1\}} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3^c\cup \{w_2\}} \sqcup\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,[9]\cup \{x,y\}}/S_2.
\end{align*}
Here, the union is over $Y_i \cup Y_i^c=[9]$, with $0 \leq |Y_1|\leq 7$, and $0 \leq |Y_j| \leq 9$ for $j=2,3$. By Lemma \ref{lem:h=s}, together with the marked point constraints established above, the separating boundary divisors with a genus $0$ component make no contribution. We proceed case by case for the remaining boundary divisors.

First, consider the boundary divisor $\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,Y_2\cup \{v_1\}} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{1,Y_2^c\cup \{v_2\}}$, \cite[Table~3]{CanningRarelyGorenstein} shows the only possible nonzero generators can occur when $|Y_2 \cup \{v_1\}|=9,10$. Furthermore in these cases,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:H15 of M3,Y2 times M1,Y2^c}
\begin{aligned}
H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,Y_2\cup \{v_1\}} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{1,Y_2^c\cup \{v_2\}})&= H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,Y_2\cup \{v_1\}}) \otimes H^0(\overline{\mathcal M}_{1,Y_2^c\cup \{v_2\}})\\
& \quad\oplus H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,Y_2\cup \{v_1\}}) \otimes H^2(\overline{\mathcal M}_{1,Y_2^c\cup \{v_2\}}).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

When \(|Y_2\cup\{v_1\}|=9\), Lemma~\ref{lem:h=s} and
Figure~\ref{fig:gentable} give
\(H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,9})=0\), while
\cite[Theorem~1.7]{CLPW24polynomial} gives
\(H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,9})=0\). To show vanishing of the pushforwards of classes in \eqref{eq:H15 of M3,Y2 times M1,Y2^c} when $|Y_2\cup \{v_1\}|=10$, we first note the following diagram commutes:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,10} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{1,1} \arrow[r, "\xi"] \arrow[d, "\eta_1 \times \mathrm{id}"']
  & \overline{\mathcal M}_{4,9} \arrow[d, "\eta_2"] \\
\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,8} \times {\overline{\mathcal M}_{1,1}} \arrow[r, "\xi"']
  & \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}
\end{tikzcd}.
\]
\noindent By the preceding section, $H^k(\overline{\mathcal M}_{4,8})=0$ for all odd $k$. Combining this with the commutative diagram above shows that the pushforward of \eqref{eq:H15 of M3,Y2 times M1,Y2^c} under the gluing map \eqref{eq:eta gluing M_4,9 to M_5,7} is zero.


We next consider the separating divisor $\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3 \cup \{w_1\} } \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3^c \cup \{w_2\} }$. By \cite[Table~3]{CanningRarelyGorenstein}, a nonzero contribution can occur only if  $|Y_3 \cup \{w_1\}|=10$ or $|Y_3^c \cup \{w_2\}|=10$. By symmetry it suffices to consider the first scenario. In this case
\begin{equation}\label{eq:H15 of M2,Y3 times M2,Y3^c}
\begin{aligned}
H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3 \cup \{w_1\} } \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3^c \cup \{w_2\} })&=H^{15}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3 \cup \{w_1\} }) \otimes H^0(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3^c \cup \{w_2\} }) \\
& \quad \oplus H^{13}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3 \cup \{w_1\} }) \otimes H^2(\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,Y_3^c \cup \{w_2\} }).\\ 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\noindent As in the preceding case, the following diagram commutes:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}
\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,10} \times \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,1} \arrow[r, "\xi"] \arrow[d, "\eta_3 \times \mathrm{id}"']
  & \overline{\mathcal M}_{4,9} \arrow[d, "\eta_2"] \\
\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,8} \times {\overline{\mathcal M}_{2,1}} \arrow[r, "\xi"']
  & \overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7}
\end{tikzcd}.
\]
Here $\eta_3: \overline{\mathcal M}_{2,10} \to \overline{\mathcal M}_{3,8}$ is the self gluing map. By \cite[Table~3]{CanningRarelyGorenstein}, $H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{3,8})=0$ for all odd $k$. Thus \eqref{eq:H15 of M2,Y3 times M2,Y3^c} vanishes under further pushforward to $H^{19}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{5,7})$ under the self gluing map \eqref{eq:eta gluing M_4,9 to M_5,7}.


